      "text": "To solve this problem, we need to determine the length of the tunnel. Let's denote the length of the tunnel by \\( L \\) miles.\n\nFirst, we know that the train is 1 mile long and is traveling at a speed of 60 miles per hour. The time it takes for the tail of the train to exit the tunnel is given as 3 minutes. We need to convert this time into hours because the speed is given in miles per hour. There are 60 minutes in an hour, so 3 minutes is:\n\n\\[\n3 \\text{ minutes} = \\frac{3}{60} \\text{ hours} = \\frac{1}{20} \\text{ hours}\n\\]\n\nDuring this time, the train travels a distance equal to its own length plus the length of the tunnel. Therefore, the distance traveled by the train in \\(\\frac{1}{20}\\) hours is:\n\n\\[\n\\text{Distance} = \\text{Speed} \\times \\text{Time} = 60 \\text{ miles per hour} \\times \\frac{1}{20} \\text{ hours} = 3 \\text{ miles}\n\\]\n\nThis distance is the sum of the length of the tunnel and the length of the train. Since the train is 1 mile long, we can write the equation:\n\n\\[\nL + 1 = 3\n\\]\n\nSolving for \\( L \\):\n\n\\[\nL = 3 - 1 = 2\n\\]\n\nThus, the length of the tunnel is \\(\\boxed{2}\\) miles.",
